==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deyḱ-}}
From late {{inh|en|enm|diccion||something said; a word or phrase}} (an obsolete sense in Modern English), borrowed directly from {{der|en|la|dictiō||a saying, speaking, uttering}} or from {{der|en|fro|dicïon||word}} ({{der|en|xno|dictyoun}}), from {{der|en|LL.|dictiō||word}}, both from {{suf|la|nocat=1|dīcō|t1=to say, to talk|tiō}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*deyḱ-||to show, to point out}}.<ref>{{R:MED|id=MED11549|entry=dicciọ̄n|pos=n}}</ref><ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED2|id=00063587}}</ref>

The modern senses of “choice and use of words” and “clarity of word choice” were likely influenced by additional senses of {{m|la|dictiō}}.<ref>{{R:L&S|dictĭo|dictio|passage='''A'''. (Good prose, for the most part only in jurid. and rhetor. lang.) {{...}} '''B'''. Kinds of delivery, style, diction {{...}} '''C'''. The use of a word or phrase, a mode of expression {{...}} '''B'''. The art of speaking, oratory}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪkʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-diction.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪkʃən|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Choice and use of words, especially with regard to effective communication.
# The effectiveness and degree of clarity of word choice and expression.
#: {{ux|en|His poor '''diction''' meant that most of the audience didn't really understand the key points of the presentation.}}
# {{lb|en|theatre}} [[enunciation|Enunciation]], [[pronunciation]].

